#   QuickJAB: JS runtime with libdog

<img align=right width="25%" src="./quickjab.jpg"/>

An anti-bloat [QuickJS][q] based JavaScript runtime with [libdog][d] bindings.
Mostly revision-control oriented: git compatibility, CRDTs, tokenizers for 70
programming languages, diff/patch/merge and other things.

### Building

QuickJAB can be built as a generic runtime or (better) with some bundled JavaScript.

 1. `build-bundled/` — the JS tree baked into the binary (JAB-035 jsrc pack); 
    the binary requires from the bundle ONLY, so it runs anywhere:

        cmake -S . -B build-bundle -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
            -GNinja -DJAB_JSRC=<path to js tree> -DJAB_BIN=<binary name> \
            -DQUICKJAB_JSRC_PACK=ON 
        ninja -C build-bundled quickjab

 2. `build-no-bundle/` — a plain runtime, no pack:

        cmake -S . -B build-no-bundle -GNinja
        ninja -C build-no-bundle quickjab

### Running

Running with no bundle (QJAB-002 require modes, first hit wins): an explicit script path (`quickjab /abs/dir/main.js`) pins `require` barewords to THAT script's dir; otherwise the first `jsrc/` at or above the cwd is the base (plant a `jsrc` symlink to your JS tree). Tests: `ctest --test-dir <builddir> -j16`; the lite suites take the runtime via `LITEJAB=<builddir>/bin/quickjab`.

With bundle present, control goes to `main.js`.

[d]: https://github.com/gritzko/libdog
[q]: https://github.com/quickjs-ng/quickjs
